#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082957 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/id #uuid "ec516fb4-a497-579c-b19d-ebc0a081eb58" :question/math-academy-id "e-5822" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u^2 +u}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{u+1} - \\dfrac{1}{3u} =\\dfrac{5}{u(u+1)}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(3u, \\:u+1, \\: u(u+1) \\big) = 3u(u+1)$.\n- The values $u=-1$ and $u=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{u + 1} - \\frac{1}{3u} &= (5)/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n3u(u + 1) \\cdot (\\frac{3}{u + 1} - \\frac{1}{3u}) &= 3u(u + 1) \\cdot (5)/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n(9u(u + 1))/(u + 1) - (3u(u + 1))/(3u) &= (15u(u + 1))/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n(9u \\cdot (u + 1))/(u + 1) - (3u \\cdot (u + 1))/(3u) &= (15 \\cdot u(u + 1))/(u(u + 1)) \\\\\n9u - (u + 1) &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $u$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9u - (u + 1) &= 15 \\\\\n9u - u - 1 &= 15 \\\\\n8u &= 16 \\\\\nu &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n2\\neq -1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n2\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $u=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $u=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082958 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/id #uuid "a578b895-ea0e-5d83-aa1d-04a2fe15f332" :question/math-academy-id "e-5824" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2} = \\dfrac{2}{k^2-5k+6}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{k-3} -\\dfrac {2}{k-2} = \\dfrac{2}{(k-2)(k-3)}\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(k-2, k-3, (k-2)(k-3) \\big) = (k-2)(k-3)$.\n- The values $k=2$ and $k=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal to zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{k - 3} - \\frac{2}{k - 2} &= (2)/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n(k - 2)(k - 3) \\cdot (\\frac{3}{k - 3} - \\frac{2}{k - 2}) &= (k - 2)(k - 3) \\cdot (2)/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n(3(k - 2)(k - 3))/(k - 3) - (2(k - 2)(k - 3))/(k - 2) &= (2(k - 2)(k - 3))/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n(3(k - 2) \\cdot (k - 3))/(k - 3) - (2 \\cdot (k - 2) \\cdot (k - 3))/(k - 2) &= (2 \\cdot (k - 2)(k - 3))/((k - 2)(k - 3)) \\\\\n3(k - 2) - 2(k - 3) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(k - 2) - 2(k - 3) &= 2 \\\\\n3k - 6 - 2k + 6 &= 2 \\\\\nk &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $k=2$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083049 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "3384b27a-d977-5421-800e-64a3581b939d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9367" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{4} {x - 2} - \\dfrac{8} {x(x - 2)} = \\dfrac {3} {x}$." :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n$\\textrm{LCM}\\big(x-2,\\: x(x-2),\\: x \\big) = x(x-2)$.\n- The values $x = 0$ and $x=2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{x - 2} - (8)/(x(x - 2)) &= \\frac{3}{x} \\\\\nx(x - 2) \\cdot (\\frac{4}{x - 2} - (8)/(x(x - 2))) &= x(x - 2) \\cdot \\frac{3}{x} \\\\\n(4x(x - 2))/(x - 2) - (8x(x - 2))/(x(x - 2)) &= (3x(x - 2))/(x) \\\\\n(4x(x - 2))/(x - 2) - (8x(x - 2))/(x(x - 2)) &= (3x(x - 2))/(x) \\\\\n4x - 8 &= 3(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 8 &= 3(x - 2) \\\\\n4x - 8 &= 3x - 6 \\\\\n4x - 3x &= -6 + 8 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=2$ is not a valid solution, we conclude that our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "805cc7ef-84c1-5dda-9496-d665ac356c56" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Trinomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016ef3cc-7d6e-56c4-8b26-7ce1164c6dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263432} {:answer/id #uuid "54a0baeb-d794-51da-83b9-446a4b0369b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263433} {:answer/id #uuid "408557e6-173c-50ce-87df-15446b7c4752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263434} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2cdf5f-d77a-5ff4-8a1e-87d171e12c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263435} {:answer/id #uuid "a198d35e-c8a2-541d-ab6f-5ee29a4dbd76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "V=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "016ef3cc-7d6e-56c4-8b26-7ce1164c6dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263432} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9599e57-b1c0-5efe-9915-6354bc652808" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "add7ad15-a989-5880-8090-8af6c15fef69" :question/math-academy-id "q-117682" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{{V}^{2}-16}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2(V+4),\\,V-4,\\,(V+4)(V-4))=2(V+4)(V-4).\n$$\n\n- The values $V=-4$ and $V=4$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4} & =\\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ 2(V+4)(V-4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}) & =2(V+4)(V-4)\\cdot \\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ \\frac{2(V+4)(V-4)}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{2(V+4)(V-4)}{V-4} & =\\frac{16(V+4)(V-4)}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2(V+4)}(V-4)}{\\cancel{2(V+4)}}+\\frac{2(V+4)\\cancel{(V-4)}}{\\cancel{V-4}} & =\\frac{16\\cancel{(V+4)(V-4)}}{\\cancel{(V+4)(V-4)}} \\\\ (V-4)+2(V+4) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(V-4)+2(V+4) & =16 \\\\ V-4+2V+8 & =16 \\\\ 3V & =12 \\\\ V & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $V=4$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c54ef1dc-a113-5a55-b347-7a225ee81a3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263471} {:answer/id #uuid "67f3020d-5728-5e8c-9d56-14299c527427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67f3020d-5728-5e8c-9d56-14299c527427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263472} :answer-field/id #uuid "e370c0f2-7b62-53d6-aa27-c9868af45366" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b8e89a0-6a6d-5b9e-b505-b40b9b084524" :question/math-academy-id "q-276459" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)}=\\frac{2}{n}.$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(n+4,\\,n(n+4),\\,n)=n(n+4).\n$$\n\n- The values $n=-4$ and $n=0$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)} & =\\frac{2}{n} \\\\ n(n+4)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{n+4}-\\frac{5}{n(n+4)}) & =n(n+4)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n} \\\\ \\frac{3n(n+4)}{n+4}-\\frac{5n(n+4)}{n(n+4)} & =\\frac{2n(n+4)}{n} \\\\ \\frac{3n\\cdot \\cancel{(n+4)}}{\\cancel{n+4}}-\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{n(n+4)}}{\\cancel{n(n+4)}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{n}\\cdot (n+4)}{\\cancel{n}} \\\\ 3n-5 & =2(n+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n-5 & =2(n+4) \\\\ 3n-5 & =2n+8 \\\\ n & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $13\\ne 0$ and $13\\ne -4,$ we conclude that $n=13$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $n=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af4db8cd-ed49-5ea9-b50e-0d053baab46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263479} {:answer/id #uuid "e14d80a2-0397-58e9-8a76-5749a1dd84d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e14d80a2-0397-58e9-8a76-5749a1dd84d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263480} :answer-field/id #uuid "682de764-2b08-508b-bebb-81cac51c8686" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "561f7a38-14ce-5b18-9a77-8971c0a37b28" :question/math-academy-id "q-276464" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2{A}^{2}+4A}.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}=\\frac{1}{2A(A+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2A,\\,A+2,\\,2A(A+2))=2A(A+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $A=0$ and $A=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2} & =\\frac{1}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ 2A(A+2)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2A}+\\frac{2}{A+2}) & =2A(A+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 2A(A+2)}{2A}+\\frac{4A(A+2)}{A+2} & =\\frac{2A(A+2)}{2A(A+2)} \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{2A}(A+2)}{\\cancel{2A}}+\\frac{4A\\cancel{(A+2)}}{\\cancel{A+2}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2A(A+2)}}{\\cancel{2A(A+2)}} \\\\ 5(A+2)+4A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving this equation for $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(A+2)+4A & =1 \\\\ 5A+10+4A & =1 \\\\ 9A & =-9 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $-1\\ne 0$ and $-1\\ne -2,$ we conclude that $A=-1$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $A=-1.$"}]}
